ndirect opening. It blocks while creating a decent structure for me.\n\nThe board is H8(B), I9(W), H7(B). Black has a vertical 2 (H7, H8). I need to block or I\'m in trouble. H9 is the best. Blocks and creates a connection with I9.\n\n**Conclusion**\n\nYep, H9 it is. No question. Blocks Black\'s vertical threat and forms a live 2 horizontally. Controls the center.\n\n**Reasoning**\n\nBlack\'s two stones (H7, H8) are the most significant thing on the board. Allowing Black to get three stones in a row would be very bad. I have to block. H9 is the best block because it connects to my existing stone and gives me a better shape.\n\nCoordinates, again, just to be sure: H9 is correct.\n\nI\'m applying the basic principles here: board survey, threat assessment, trying to find winning moves, and blocking the threat while improving my position. H9 covers all those bases.\n\nThere are no diagonal threats I need to worry about right now.\n\n**Final Answer**\n\nH9. That\'s the move.\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: The game is in the opening phase. Black (X) has formed a vertical "Live Two" with stones at H7 and H8. This is an immediate threat because if Black is allowed to play at H9 or H6 on the next turn, they will form a "Live Three" (open on both ends), which is a very strong attacking position that often leads to a win. White (O) has a single stone at I9. White must prioritize blocking Black\'s development while trying to build their own structure.\n\nReason: To neutralize Black\'s threat, White should block the vertical line. The candidate moves for blocking are H6 and H9. H9 is the significantly better choice because it blocks Black\'s extension at the top while being horizontally adjacent to White\'s existing stone at I9. This creates a "Live Two" for White (H9-I9), turning a defensive move into a constructive one that builds White\'s own offensive potential.\n\nAnswer: {"col": "H", "row": 9}'}
